## Research Interests

My research interests in economic history began with my anxiety of becoming China's "lost generation" similar to Japan's Heisei Generation when I was an undergraduate student. I wanted to examine economic cycles and financial crises, hoping to understand where my generation stood in history. As it turned out, reality answered my question before my courses did. 

Nonetheless, I stayed in economic history and developed a deeper interest in the financial history of Hong Kong. Hong Kong is a fascinating place where international banking and traditional Chinese finance coexisted and evolved together for over a century. What fascinates me is how a small port transformed into one of the world's leading financial centres, especially through the interplay between global capital flows, merchant networks, and colonial governance. Understanding this process speaks to broader questions about how financial systems develop, adapt, and connect across borders. 

